## qhypstub requirements, not a Ferrari security claim

At the qhypstub pin, README.md:1-50 names MSM8916/APQ8016 and MSM8939 as
known-working SoCs. It is a firmware stub, not a hypervisor.

Firmware secure boot is explicitly distinct from Android bootloader unlocking.
Replacing HYP requires firmware replacement to be permitted; unlocked Android
boot images do not establish that. The README says to assume secure boot enabled
if uncertain. A separate runtime loader relies on validation gaps in SOME
Qualcomm TZ calls, not universal firmware behavior or permission to experiment.
This investigation establishes no installed-phone fuse or TZ vulnerability state.

README.md:27-34: a primary AArch64 bootloader can enter EL2; AArch32 LK enters
EL1. Its AArch64 OS reaches EL2 only with the HVC boot patch. Otherwise it
enters EL1 even if qhypstub is present.
README-linked implementation commits, verified publicly in official lk2nd:
- 8d840ad94c60f1f5ab0a95e886839454e03d8b86, "Try jumping to aarch64 kernel in
  EL2 using hypervisor call", platform/msm_shared/scm.c and include/scm.h.
- 4562184e245f0fdc429fd13600187090eed3e20d, runtime-loader introduction.

At qhypstub.s:227-298 the stub invokes TZ state-switch SMC and traps back into
EL2 to avoid the documented secondary-CPU execution-state problem.
README and qhypstub.ld require protecting 4 KiB at 0x86400000. These are source
requirements, not instructions to modify this phone's memory layout or firmware.

## Why both lk2nd pins matter

Legacy target/msm8916/qhypstub_loader.c:116-213 implements runtime loading;
platform/msm_shared/scm.c:1052-1058 has HVC-first boot with SMC fallback.
lk2nd/smp/cpu-boot.c:82-174 handles boot addresses and core/cluster-L2 startup.
lk2nd/smp/spin-table.c:160-253 sets qhypstub's AArch64 state BEFORE starting
secondaries so their exception level remains consistent.

Current main has no qhypstub-named loader and its inspected scm_elexec_call in
platform/msm_shared/scm.c:1050-1085 lacks the HVC-first logic. Do not attribute
the old README's runtime loader/EL2 path to whichever branch is named main.

Ferrari is explicitly listed in legacy README.md:103 and current
Documentation/devices.md:72. Its device entry is nested in:
- legacy dts/msm8916/msm8939-mtp.dts:35-49;
- current lk2nd/device/dts/msm8916/msm8939-mtp.dts:64-81, with unresolved
  lk2nd,dtb-files FIXME.
Device recognition and implementation capability are not proof of a complete
Ferrari mainline DTB, working eight-core handoff, display or USB on this phone.

## Mainline CPU contract versus pinned Pocketboot

Linux baseline arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8939.dtsi defines eight CPUs,
MPIDRs 0x100..0x103 and 0x0..0x3, boot CPU0x100, all using spin-table with
placeholder release addresses requiring a second-stage shim.
Documentation/arch/arm64/booting.rst requires consistent entry exception level,
cache/MMU/register state, coherency, reserved spin-table memory and aligned
initially-zero 64-bit release slots. Release addresses are little-endian.
The earlier live downstream qcom,arm-cortex-acc configuration is a different
boot interface; it does not prove this mainline contract has been supplied.

Pinned Pocketboot is four-core MSM8916, not an eight-core MSM8939 implementation:
- pocketpreboot/src/msm8916.rs:12-25: MAX_CPUS=4.
- pocketpreboot/src/msm8916.rs:583-630: collect_cpus rejects MPIDRs >=4,
  requires exactly four CPUs and fixed MSM8916 ACC geometry.
- pocketpreboot/src/spin_table.S:35-46: resident secondaries accept affinities1-3.
- src/kexec.rs:1431-1444: kexec contract requires MPIDRs0-3.
- patches/kernel/msm8916/0001-arm64-pocketboot-spin-table-kexec.patch:298-319:
  parking path rejects is_hyp_mode_available() and requires secondary EL1.
- docs/msm8916-spin-table-abi.md:17-28 specifies four CPUs, CPU0 affinity and
  ownership/preservation of the release slots.
git grep found no msm8939/ferrari references in this source snapshot.
Inference from these explicit checks: simply increasing NR_CPUS cannot make
this topology/ABI implementation support the MSM8939 handoff. This is not a
claim that every possible external boot path requires that same ABI.

